summary: The machine address of an abi::bytes::Bytes payload as an x86-64 register word, for a raw syscall; native backends only (interp and compile fail with NATIVE-ONLY).
index: stdlib
symbol: abi::x86_64::from_bytes
keywords: buffer address; pointer to bytes; raw address; syscall argument
related: stdlib.module.abi::x86_64 backend.native-only stdlib.linux::abi::x86_64::syscall stdlib.abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes stdlib.module.abi::bytes

== SIGNATURE
    abi::x86_64::from_bytes(struct) -> struct

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace abi::x86_64: import abi::x86_64, then write
abi::x86_64::from_bytes. Method syntax: receiver.from_bytes(...) is the
same call.

== BACKENDS
native backends only (cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); interp
and compile refuse the call at run time with NATIVE-ONLY

== NOTES
Returns an abi::x86_64::Register64 holding the address of the first byte
of the Bytes' storage (a valid one-past address for an empty one): the
value a register argument of linux::abi::x86_64::syscall carries. The
argument must statically be an abi::bytes::Bytes, and the bridge is only
ever called: binding it as a function value is a TYPE error ("can only be
called directly"). The native lowering keeps the storage alive until the
syscall that consumes the address has returned. The address is read-only
for the kernel: a buffer it writes needs from_mutable_bytes.

A machine address exists only on the native backends (cranelift,
cranelift-generic, executables) on Linux x86-64; the Tcl backends refuse
the call when it runs (NATIVE-ONLY) and imitate nothing. It is not a
direct operation by itself -- the syscall that uses the address is.
Ordinary programs never need it: linux::write and io::print_line call it
for them.

@topic stdlib.abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes
title: abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes -- intrinsic of namespace abi::x86_64
summary: The machine address of an abi::bytes::MutableBytes payload as an x86-64 register word, for a syscall that writes the buffer; native backends only (NATIVE-ONLY elsewhere).
index: stdlib
symbol: abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes
keywords: writable buffer address; raw address; read buffer
related: stdlib.module.abi::x86_64 backend.native-only stdlib.abi::x86_64::from_bytes stdlib.mutable_byte_store::detach

== SIGNATURE
    abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes(struct) -> struct

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace abi::x86_64: import abi::x86_64, then write
abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes. Method syntax:
receiver.from_mutable_bytes(...) is the same call.

== BACKENDS
native backends only (cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); interp
and compile refuse the call at run time with NATIVE-ONLY

== NOTES
The writable twin of abi::x86_64::from_bytes: the same contract, but the
argument must statically be an abi::bytes::MutableBytes and the kernel
may write through the address. It does not copy: it hands out the
address of the storage it is given, so the caller must pass storage no
other value observes -- linux::read does, by detaching a private copy
first (abi::bytes::detach) and returning that copy as the result's data.
That is the raw layer's sharp edge; use linux::read or linux::io instead.

@topic stdlib.module.abi::x86_64
title: abi::x86_64 -- standard namespace
summary: The x86-64 register word abi::x86_64::Register64: conversions from Int and ABI integers, the signed reading back, and the native-only address bridges.
index: stdlib
symbol: abi::x86_64
keywords: register; x86-64; syscall arguments; machine word
related: stdlib.linux::abi::x86_64::syscall stdlib.module.abi stdlib.module.abi::aarch64

== DESCRIPTION
Register64 is one 64-bit x86-64 register's contents, and nothing else: a bit
pattern carried across an architecture boundary (today, only
linux::abi::x86_64::syscall's register inputs and its rax result). It is
ordinary Botlish: a nominal struct whose one field holds the register's
contents under the signed (two's-complement) reading, typed with the
source-defined domain Register64Word below. That field type is what keeps
the value sound with no privileged code at all: a construction `Register64
{word: x}` anywhere must statically prove x in the domain (STRUCTS.md,
"Named struct construction": no implicit runtime cast, no guard), so no
Register64 can hold anything but exactly one of the 2^64 register bit
patterns.

It is transport, not a machine-integer programming model: Register64 is a
struct, so it has no arithmetic, ordering, bitwise or pointer operations (`r
+ 5` is the ordinary TYPE error any struct operand gets), no wrapping, no
promotion and no C integer semantics. The only ways in are the conversions
below (register64 from an Int, from_* from an ABI numeric value); the only
way out is to_int.

  abi::x86_64::register64(value: int)   Int -> Register64, the checked
                        conversion: exactly -2^63 <= value <= 2^63 - 1 is
                        accepted, and its register contents are that
                        value's 64-bit two's-complement bit pattern (so
                        -1 is 0xffffffffffffffff). Anything else is the
                        declared error Register64BelowRange /
                        Register64AboveRange -- never truncation. The
                        call-specific completion proof (STATIC-COMPLETION-
                        PROOFS.md) makes a provably in-range argument
                        (register64(39)) need no handler, and a provably
                        out-of-range literal a compile-time KNOWN-ERROR.
  abi::x86_64::to_int(register)         Register64 -> Int: the register
                        contents read as a signed 64-bit two's-complement
                        integer (0x27 -> 39, 0xffffffffffffffff -> -1), an
                        ordinary arbitrary-precision Int (statically known
                        to be a Register64Word). Representation only: no
                        errno or other meaning is read into it.

  abi::x86_64::from_i8(value) ... from_usize(value)
                        ABI numeric value -> Register64 (ABI-NUMERIC-
                        DOMAINS.md): total, no error, no check. Signed
                        values (abi::I8 I16 I32 I64 Isize) are sign-
                        extended to 64 bits, unsigned ones (abi::U8 U16
                        U32 U64 Usize) zero-extended -- see "Encoding ABI
                        numeric values" below.

Why the inverse lives here and not in a portable `abi` namespace: the signed
64-bit reading is a property of this architecture's register word, and
Botlish has no overloading, so a portable abi::to_int(Register64) would tie
`abi` to x86-64 (another architecture's register type could not be accepted
without redefining it). Each architecture's transport layer owns its own
register type and its conversions.

The x86-64-specific *semantics* (that a register is 64 bits, read signed)
are all here, in source. The only privileged operation is the kernel
transition itself, linux::abi::x86_64::syscall (core/linuxabi.tcl, the
native backend's helper in native/src/runtime/syscall.rs), which takes and
returns Register64 values and nothing else. lib/abi/aarch64.bot is the same
layer for AArch64 (LINUX-SYSCALL-TRANSPORTS.md).

== SOURCE
lib/abi/x86_64.bot (import abi::x86_64).

== MEMBERS
    abi::x86_64::Register64        struct
    abi::x86_64::Register64AboveRange error
    abi::x86_64::Register64BelowRange error -- The two declared errors of t...
    abi::x86_64::Register64Word    type -- The Ints one 64-bit register wor...
    abi::x86_64::from_bytes        intrinsic -- The machine address of an a...
    abi::x86_64::from_i16          function
    abi::x86_64::from_i32          function
    abi::x86_64::from_i64          function
    abi::x86_64::from_i8           function
    abi::x86_64::from_isize        function
    abi::x86_64::from_mutable_bytes intrinsic -- The machine address of an ...
    abi::x86_64::from_u16          function
    abi::x86_64::from_u32          function
    abi::x86_64::from_u64          function -- The upper half, 2^63 ..
    abi::x86_64::from_u8           function
    abi::x86_64::from_usize        function
    abi::x86_64::register64        function -- Int -> Register64.
    abi::x86_64::to_int            function -- Register64 -> Int

