integer refinement *is* (a named subtype of Int, or of another such subtype,
whose values are exactly an inclusive interval or a finite set of integers);
this file is what says Byte/Nibble/LowNibble/HighNibble
*are* ones, and what their own domains are. No downstream machinery
(result-contract verification, range/exact-set analysis, checked
constructors, native lowering) treats these four names specially: everything
below is the same generic feature any other source-defined "type Child =
Parent in Domain" declaration gets.

  Byte        0..255                a plain 8-bit value
  Nibble      0..15                 a logical 4-bit value
  LowNibble   0..15                 a nibble already positioned in the low
                                    half of a Byte (numerically identical
                                    to Nibble's domain, but a distinct,
                                    nominal type: LowNibble means "already
                                    positioned", Nibble means "logical
                                    value" -- see item 6/74 of the report)
  HighNibble  {0,16,32,...,240}     a nibble positioned in the high half of
                                    a Byte -- 16 exact values, not every
                                    integer in [0,240]

== NOTES
A Byte is an Int, so every Int operation applies, and its arithmetic is
Int arithmetic (a sum of two Bytes is an Int, not a wrapped Byte). An Int
enters the domain where the compiler proves 0 <= x <= 255 -- a literal, a
comparison guard, a function's declared `-> Byte` result -- or through
byte::from_int, which fails otherwise. str::encode_utf8 returns
List[Byte]; abi::bytes::from_list takes one.

