@topic stdlib.decimal_rounded::round
title: decimal_rounded::round -- intrinsic of namespace decimal_rounded
summary: decimal_rounded::round[T](x): the value of the DecimalRounded type T whose decimal is the Float64 x rounded half to even to T's places, after `import decimal_rounded`.
index: stdlib
symbol: decimal_rounded::round
keywords: round to places; quantize; float to decimal; make decimal; banker's rounding
related: stdlib.module.decimal_rounded concept.decimal-rounded stdlib.float64::from_decimal_rounded error.decimal-guarantee-lost

== SIGNATURE
    decimal_rounded::round(Float64) -> DecimalRounded

== ERRORS
    UnrepresentableDecimal

A call must handle each (on NAME:), admit it in its function's errors
clause, or be proven unable to raise it (rule.unhandled-errors).

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace decimal_rounded: import decimal_rounded, then
write decimal_rounded::round. Method syntax: receiver.round(...) is the
same call.

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
The one way to mint a guarantee from a value computed at run time: it
does not claim x already is a decimal, it rounds it. T is imprinted
explicitly and must be a refined DecimalRounded type (decimal_rounded::
round[Float64](x) is a TYPE error, and without [T] it is IMPRINT). The
rounding reads x's exact binary value: the Float64 1.005 is
1.00499999999999989..., so it rounds to 1.00 at two places. The result is
represented to satisfy every place up to N; when no representation does
-- a decimal finer than binary64 holds at its magnitude, such as
4873863637.854281 at nine places -- the call fails with the declared
error UnrepresentableDecimal. Runs on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import decimal_rounded

fn cents(x: Float64) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2} errors UnrepresentableDecimal:
    decimal_rounded::round[DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}](x)

fn safe(x: Float64) -> DecimalRounded{minDecimalsPreserved: 2}:
    x.cents():
        on UnrepresentableDecimal:
            0.00

[safe(0.1 + 0.2), safe(1.005), safe(-0.125)]
@value [0.30, 1.00, -0.12]
@end

