@topic stdlib.module.io
title: io -- standard namespace
summary: Portable text output -- io::print, print_line, write_error and write_error_line -- through the io::IO context trait; with linux::io it runs on the native backends.
index: stdlib
symbol: io
keywords: print; output; stdout; stderr; write text; console
related: task.print-output backend.io concept.context-traits

== DESCRIPTION
  io::print(text)              TEXT on the environment's standard output
  io::print_line(text)         TEXT and a newline, as one operation
  io::write_error(text)        TEXT on the environment's error output
  io::write_error_line(text)   TEXT and a newline on the error output

Each succeeds (unit) or fails with WriteFailed; none exposes a descriptor, a
byte count, a buffer, a raw syscall result or an errno. The caller writes no
context: the operations declare `context io: IO`, the compiler derives that
every function calling them requires IO, and the program installs one
context that implements IO before the first call that needs it:

  import io
  import linux::io

  fn hello():
      io::print_line("Hello, world!")

  with context linux::io::create()
  hello()

IO is a *context trait*: a set of operations an installed context supplies,
never a value. A concrete context implements it structurally, in its own
namespace -- linux::io::LinuxIO through linux::io::write_text and
linux::io::write_error_text -- with no registration and no `implements`.
Where a call needs IO the compiler selects, statically, the one installed
context that implements it (two would be AMBIGUOUS-CONTEXT-IMPLEMENTATION,
none MISSING-CONTEXT), and compiles `io.write_text(...)` below to a direct
call of that context's implementation: no dispatch, no hidden argument, no
provider table at run time.

Not here (deliberately): input, files, paths, buffering, a byte-count or
errno API. Buffering, batching and scratch storage can live behind these
operations later without any caller changing.

== SOURCE
lib/io.bot (import io).

== MEMBERS
    io::IO                         context trait -- The environment's text ...
    io::WriteFailed                error -- A portable output operation did...
    io::print                      function -- TEXT on the standard output.
    io::print_line                 function -- TEXT followed by a newline o...
    io::write_error                function -- TEXT on the error output.
    io::write_error_line           function -- TEXT followed by a newline o...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help io::IO).

== NOTES
The operations require the io::IO context trait, which a program
satisfies by installing a context that implements it, normally
`with context linux::io::create()`. That standard implementation writes
with raw Linux syscalls, so printing runs only on the native backends
(cranelift, cranelift-generic, executables); on interp and compile the
first write fails with NATIVE-ONLY. On every backend the program's final
value is still printed by main.tcl. There is no input, file or
formatting API here.

