@topic stdlib.module.linux::io
title: linux::io -- standard namespace
summary: The process's standard input, output and error as one context, LinuxIO: install it with `with context linux::io::create()`; it implements io::IO.
index: stdlib
symbol: linux::io
keywords: stdout; stdin; stderr; standard output; console io
related: stdlib.module.io stdlib.module.linux concept.contexts concept.context-traits

== DESCRIPTION
  with context linux::io::create()        install it (entry program)
  linux::io::write(data)                  write(2) to the context's stdout
  linux::io::write_error(data)            write(2) to the context's stderr
  linux::io::read(buffer)                 read(2) from the context's stdin

LinuxIO is ONE context, the process I/O environment, holding the three
standard descriptors; it is not three contexts. A function that uses it says
so once, in its context section (`context io: LinuxIO`), and is called with
its ordinary arguments only: `linux::io::write(data)`. Code between the
entry program and such a function declares nothing -- the compiler derives
that it requires LinuxIO -- and a call made before `with context
linux::io::create()` is rejected at compile time (MISSING-CONTEXT, with the
call chain).

Capability boundary (DIRECT-MODULES.md). `linux` is a direct module: its
write and read take any descriptor, raw process authority. This module is
not: every function here that calls into `linux` requires LinuxIO, the
context it defines itself, so the raw authority reaches its callers only as
that explicit capability -- and nothing above linux::io is direct for using
it.

This module is one level above `linux` (lib/linux.bot): every operation here
is the existing raw-thin wrapper applied to a descriptor taken from the
context, and every external effect still flows

  linux::io::write --> linux::write --> linux::sys::write --> the target
  architecture's transport (linux::abi::x86_64::syscall: `syscall`;
  linux::abi::aarch64::syscall: `svc #0`)

with no libc, no compiler knowledge of write(2) and no new syscall code. The
low-level layer stays as it is: code that wants an explicit descriptor calls
linux::write(fd, data) / linux::read(fd, buffer) directly.

Representation authority. FileDescriptor and LinuxIO are opaque
(OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md): only this module may construct them or look inside.
Outside it a LinuxIO -- even one a function receives as its context -- can
be held and passed but not taken apart (`io.stdout` is
OPAQUE-REPRESENTATION): being a context grants no representation authority.
Inside, the consumers below project `io.stdout.raw` directly; there are
deliberately no public descriptor accessors.

FileDescriptor's theorem is narrow: "this value is a Linux file-descriptor
token minted by the owning Linux I/O abstraction". It does not claim the
descriptor is open, or stays open. The machine token is an abi::I32 (so the
value is proven to be in the signed 32-bit domain); application code gets no
.raw, no integer conversion and no address. Minting needs no special
mechanism: the opaque representation already makes this module the only code
that can build one, so create() mints the three standard tokens 0, 1, 2 --
constants the existing completion proofs show are in abi::I32's domain, with
no run-time range check.

LinuxIO implements the portable io::IO context trait (CONTEXT-TRAITS.md)
with write_text and write_error_text below: ordinary functions of this
module whose one context parameter is a LinuxIO -- the implicit receiver --
and whose signatures match io::IO's requirements. Nothing registers them:
where a call needs io::IO and LinuxIO is the one installed context that
implements it, the compiler binds `io.write_text(...)` to a direct call of
linux::io::write_text, which loads its descriptor from LinuxIO's own context
slot as every function here does.

Not here (deliberately): errno mapping beyond io::WriteFailed (the raw
write/read below return the kernel's signed values), buffering,
close/ownership/RAII (the standard descriptors need no application cleanup),
other descriptors (openat, sockets), mutable contexts.

== SOURCE
lib/linux/io.bot (import linux::io).

== MEMBERS
    linux::io::FileDescriptor      opaque struct -- A Linux file-descriptor...
    linux::io::LinuxIO             opaque context struct -- The process I/O...
    linux::io::create              function -- The standard process I/O env...
    linux::io::read                function -- read(2) from the context's s...
    linux::io::write               function -- write(2) of DATA to the cont...
    linux::io::write_all           function -- Writes every byte of BYTES t...
    linux::io::write_error         function -- write(2) of DATA to the cont...
    linux::io::write_error_text    function -- All of TEXT, UTF-8 encoded, ...
    linux::io::write_text          function -- All of TEXT, UTF-8 encoded, ...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help linux::io::FileDescriptor).

== NOTES
The functions here require the LinuxIO context and take no descriptor:
write, write_error and read, plus write_text and write_error_text, which
make LinuxIO the implementation of the portable io::IO trait. Calling one
before the context is installed is MISSING-CONTEXT. Not a direct module:
it mediates linux's raw authority through its own context. Its writes
are raw syscalls, so they run on the native backends only.

