A call must handle each (on NAME:), admit it in its function's errors
clause, or be proven unable to raise it (rule.unhandled-errors).

== USE
A language primitive: visible everywhere, no import.

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
argv() is an ordinary call of a language primitive (no import): it returns
a List[str] whose element 0 is the invocation name the launching process
supplied (not necessarily a path to the executable), followed by the
arguments. Every call of one run sees the same arguments.

Each argument is validated as strict UTF-8 when argv() is called; one
invalid argument fails the whole call with the builtin declared error
InvalidArgumentEncoding (no partial List, no lossy decoding). So a call
must handle it (`on InvalidArgumentEncoding:`), admit it in its
function's errors clause, or let it reach top level, which is rejected as
UNHANDLED-ERROR. The error carries no payload.

Running under main.tcl, -argv TEXT appends one argument (the first one is
argument zero), -argv-hex HEX one given as bytes, and -argv-none gives an
empty vector; with none of them, argv() is ["botlish-runner"]. A native
executable reads its real process arguments. It works on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import list

args = argv():
    on InvalidArgumentEncoding:
        []
args.length() >= 1
@caption Handle the encoding error
@value true
@end

