== DESCRIPTION
Runs the suite once and returns its report: every result's lines, in
registration order, then the summary line. A run that reports a different
number of results than there are registered cases is itself a failure,
reported as one more failed line ("FAIL the run"): the runner is ordinary
Botlish over an ordinary coroutine, and a fault below it (a coroutine that
lost a segment) must not shorten a report silently.

@topic stdlib.module.test
title: test -- standard namespace
summary: The golden test framework, an ordinary Botlish library: a suite context, test::value, test::holds and test::rejects cases, and test::report.
index: stdlib
symbol: test
keywords: testing; unit tests; test framework; assertions; golden tests
related: concept.testing concept.negative-tests

== DESCRIPTION
A golden test runs an operation, observes its outcome, compares the outcome
with an expected one, and records the comparison as data. There is no test
syntax, no assertion, no test-only native and no hidden registry: a suite is
an ordinary context value holding an ordinary MutableVector, registration is
an ordinary call that pushes a deferred check into it, and the runner is an
ordinary coroutine that runs the checks in registration order and yields
their results.

  import compiler
  import test

  fn register_arithmetic_tests():
      fn adds() errors compiler::CompilerError:
          static compile:
              20 + 22
      test::value("adds", 42, adds)
      fn unbound() errors compiler::CompilerError:
          static compile:
              missing + 1
      test::rejects("unbound", compiler::DiagnosticCode::Unbound, unbound)

  with context test::suite()
  register_arithmetic_tests()
  test::report()          # ["PASS adds", "PASS unbound", "2 passed, 0 failed"]

The suite. `test::suite()` is the value a program installs with `with
context`: a Suite whose one field is the MutableVector of registered cases.
The context value itself is an ordinary immutable struct, installed once
like any context (entry program top level only, at most one per program,
CONTEXTS.md); registration mutates the vector it holds
(`suite.cases.push(...)`), never the binding -- the place model of
MUTABLE-VECTOR.md. Calling a registration function where no suite is
installed is the ordinary MISSING-CONTEXT.

Registration. `test::case(name, check)` records a TestCase {name, run} whose
`run` is a function value of the one uniform type Fn{args: [], return:
Match}: registration stores it and never calls it. A function value is the
deferred representation because Botlish coroutines start eagerly --
`coroutine {step, first} = f()` runs f to its first yield at once
(COROUTINES.md 15) -- so storing a started coroutine would run the test
while registering it. The value helpers below (`test::value`, `test::holds`,
`test::rejects`, ...) wrap a test body into such a check while its concrete
types are known: they call the body, turn the one error a body may complete
with, compiler::CompilerError, into a mismatch, compare, and return a Match.
A body is a function of no arguments whose only declared error is
compiler::CompilerError: any other error the body may raise must be handled
inside it (the compiler rejects the registration otherwise, at the call: the
body's error set does not fit the parameter contract `Fn{args: [], return:
any, errors: [compiler::CompilerError]}` the helper's code infers). Botlish
has no catch-all handler, so this is how an arbitrary test outcome becomes
data without one.

Matchers. A Match is the verdict of one comparison, as data: {matched,
expected, actual, detail}, the three texts saying what was expected, what
was observed and anything further (a diagnostic's message). A mismatch is a
value, never an error: a failing test does not stop the run. `test::equal`
compares with `==`; `test::satisfies` applies a caller's predicate;
`test::diagnostic` compares a rejected block's compiler::DiagnosticCode
(never its message); `test::passed()` and `test::mismatch(...)` build
verdicts for a custom check.

Running. `test::run()` is the runner coroutine: started with `coroutine
{step, first} = test::run()`, it runs one case per segment, in registration
order, and sends that case's result as a one-element List; its last segment
sends the empty List and completes. (A coroutine's yields and its return
share one outward type and Botlish has no sum type with a payload, so "one
more result" and "no more results" are a List of one result and the empty
List: a batch of results.) Every start runs every registered case again: a
suite may be run any number of times, each run calling each check exactly
once. `test::results()` drains a run, `test::report()` formats it: one "PASS
name" line per passing case, a "FAIL name" line and its indented
"expected:", "actual:" and "detail:" lines per failing one, then "P passed,
F failed".

Text. Botlish has no Int-to-String conversion and no way to make a String
from a character (LANGUAGE-LIBRARY-DEFICIENCIES.md 3 and 11), so this module
writes its own (`int_text`, `describe`, slices of one table for its
punctuation), and an enum has no String form (ENUMS.md), so `code_name`
spells every compiler::DiagnosticCode case itself.

