@topic stdlib.module.web
title: web -- standard namespace
summary: URI percent-encoding (web::uri_escape_text) and the refinement types web::Emailish and web::UriQueryValue with their proving predicates.
index: stdlib
symbol: web
keywords: url encoding; percent encoding; uri escape; email validation; web
related: concept.refinements stdlib.module.str

== DESCRIPTION
uri_escape_text is the executable percent-encoding algorithm NATIVE-URI-
ESCAPE.md moved out of lib/web.tcl's uriEscape native -native-body (raw core
IR, pasted into every call site) and into this ordinary cross-file Botlish
module definition, so it is resolved and compiled once, like any other
function, instead of copied. It is the same algorithm as the removed
lib/web.tcl native's Tcl implementation: percent-encode every UTF-8 byte of
s except the RFC 3986 unreserved characters (A-Za-z0-9._~-), as uppercase
%XX. It reads s one character at a time with str::char_at (core/strings.tcl:
str -> UnicodeChar, no allocation) and char::scalar_value, and writes a
non-ASCII scalar's UTF-8 bytes from the scalar with bit arithmetic
(esc_scalar), so it builds neither a one-character String per position nor a
str::encode_utf8 List per character (ENCODE-UTF8-ALLOCATION-RESEARCH.md).

high_nibble extracts the high half of a byte and normalizes it to a logical
nibble via the Byte/Nibble/HighNibble scalar domains and typed bit
arithmetic (core/scalarbits.tcl, lib/byte.bot; see
BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md): byte::high_nibble(b) is the position-
preserving extraction (b & 0xF0), and byte::nibble(...) normalizes that to
the logical 0..15 value hex_pair/hex_digits index with.

is_unreserved (ASCII-AND-URI-UNRESERVED.md) is now the compositional form
the earlier milestones' own comments already anticipated: ASCII letters or
digits (ascii::is_alphanumeric, lib/ascii.bot -- structural fact, owned by
the "ascii" namespace) OR membership in additional_unreserved_chars, the
four-byte '-' '.' '_' '~' set RFC 3986 additionally exempts from
percent-encoding (URI *policy*, owned here, never folded into ascii::'s own
structural classes). The set is built once, from byte::set(['-', '.', '_',
'~']) (lib/byte.bot, BYTE-SET.md/MINIMAL-IMMUTABLE-SET.md's
retained-module-value machinery), and reused by every is_unreserved call --
never reconstructed per call. This is now the clean, handler-free
initializer STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md restores: the call-specific
completion proof (hir/completions.tcl) proves every element of this literal
list is statically in Byte's own domain, so byte::set's own declared
AboveRange error is proven impossible for this exact call -- no handler, no
placeholder fallback set, no `on` clause.

is_unreserved's callers pass it an ASCII scalar value as a Byte with no
conversion: char::scalar_value's result is nonnegative and each call is
guarded by `v <= 127` (an `and` operand or an `if` branch), so the range
analysis proves 0..127, inside Byte's domain (no byte::from_int, no
handler).

esc_from/esc_scalar accumulate their result by repeated concat, the same
recursive idiom examples/stdlib/string_reverse.bot's reverse_from and
examples/stdlib/ai_text_clean.bot's clean_from already use.

Emailish and UriQueryValue are this module's refinement types
(REFINEMENT-VALUES.md): ordinary Botlish declarations, `refined type NAME =
str`, each a new nominal type whose values are exactly Strings proven to
satisfy its proposition, with the exact runtime representation of str (no
wrapper, tag or allocation). This module is their owner: only its own
functions may declare a proof of them (`proves`), and each has exactly one
such function here, emailish? and uri_query_value?. Code anywhere may call
them, pass a proven value where the refinement is required, and use a
refined value wherever a str is expected; nothing outside this module can
forge the proof. The compiler knows neither name: every fact it derives
about them comes from these declarations.

